WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.220
Submilli is a runtime for code written by
agents.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.180
It runs useful programs while enforcing
controls outside

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:08.938
the model.

00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:13.947
Packages expose the operations that code
can use.

00:00:14.067 --> 00:00:17.627
Their authors place explicit permission
checks before protected

00:00:17.707 --> 00:00:18.234
work.

00:00:19.567 --> 00:00:23.407
You define and approve a blueprint: the
available packages

00:00:23.487 --> 00:00:25.087
and permitted operations.

00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:28.287
Your application supplies trusted context.

00:00:28.687 --> 00:00:31.034
The runtime enforces the rules.

00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:34.847
Let’s total charges for Customer A.

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:38.447
This illustrative blueprint allows the
billing lookup only

00:00:38.527 --> 00:00:41.167
for the customer supplied by the
application.

00:00:41.407 --> 00:00:43.567
Here, that is Customer A.

00:00:43.647 --> 00:00:46.656
Other protected operations are denied by
default.

00:00:47.967 --> 00:00:51.567
The agent writes this program in
Submilli’s curated subset

00:00:51.647 --> 00:00:52.527
of TypeScript.

00:00:53.007 --> 00:00:55.567
It reads the charges and totals their
amounts.

00:00:56.127 --> 00:01:00.447
Unsupported APIs and unavailable imports
fail validation

00:01:00.527 --> 00:01:01.807
before execution.

00:01:02.367 --> 00:01:04.633
Valid code still needs permission.

00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:09.293
Before reading the local billing ledger,
the package checks

00:01:09.333 --> 00:01:11.453
the operation and customer ID.

00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:13.933
The runtime applies the blueprint.

00:01:14.413 --> 00:01:17.662
Customer A matches, so the lookup
proceeds.

00:01:18.967 --> 00:01:20.727
Now request Customer B.

00:01:21.287 --> 00:01:24.727
The code still passes language validation,
but the permission

00:01:24.887 --> 00:01:26.407
check denies this lookup.

00:01:26.887 --> 00:01:31.323
Customer B does not match the
application’s Customer A context.

00:01:32.633 --> 00:01:36.873
Customer A’s program returns two charges,
totaling six thousand

00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:39.113
one hundred and fifty cents.

00:01:39.193 --> 00:01:42.037
Useful code runs with the access you
approved.
